"><span class=\"adwhen\">10:00:04 · step 7</span> <b>received from Quality Reviewer (Audit)</b><p>All criteria pass; checklist may hold.\nFindings (code tally): 12 PASS. Workable (code): no, 3 problem(s).</p></div><div class=\"admsg\" data-msg=\"46\"><span class=\"adwhen\">10:00:04 · step 7</span> <b>received from Quality Reviewer (Audit)</b><p>All criteria pass; checklist may hold.\nFindings (code tally): 12 PASS. Workable (code): no, 3 problem(s).</p></div></details></section><section class=\"adsec\"><h4>Thinking</h4><details class=\"adcall\" data-call=\"1\" open><summary>Call 1 · step 1 · cycle 0 · 0 s · 10:00:04</summary><div class=\"adrow\"><b>Was given</b> the chat (1 messages), its task</div><div class=\"adrow\"><b>Reason</b> <p>Scripted mock reply: no model reasoning.</p></div><div class=\"adrow\"><b>Reply</b> <p class=\"empty\">(sent as its message to You (the Requester))</p><div class=\"adfield\"><span class=\"empty\">to agents:</span> <span class=\"empty\">(sent as its message to Strategist (Adaptation))</span></div><div class=\"adfield\"><span class=\"empty\">enough:</span> true</div><div class=\"adfield\"><span class=\"empty\">hands over to:</span> Adaptation</div><div class=\"adfield\"><span class=\"empty\">goal:</span> 721 characters (in the technical details)</div></div><details class=\"adtech\"><summary>Show technical details</summary><div class=\"adrow\"><b>system text</b><pre class=\"arttext\">&lt;!-- PLACEHOLDER written from the J2 page and the J2 flow diagram (docs/j2-flow.jpg). Replace with the official file when it arrives. --&gt;\n# Department rules (every function follows these)\n\nYou are one of five functions running one department in Native: Identity, Adaptation, Priority,\nCoordination and Audit. Each registered workflow gets its own workflow agent (&quot;W-1 Builder&quot;, &quot;W-2 Builder&quot;, ...)\nthat runs only that workflow and owns one marked section of the site file. A code validator checks the charter and each\nworkflow before it is registered. The Requester (a person) gives the brief, answers Identity's questions and\nmay ask for changes at the end. There is no CEO: where a role file says &quot;ask the CEO&quot;, Identity asks the\nRequester instead, and no other function may. The department's job right now is to build the website the\nRequester asked for.\n\n## How we work\n- Everyone reads the whole chat and the current goal before speaking. Keep messages short: 1-3 sentences.\n- Address others by @name: @Identity, @Adaptation, @Priority, @Coordination, @Audit, or a workflow agent\n  by its id (@W-1).\n- Only Identity talks to the Requester (@Requester). If you need something from the Requester, tell\n  @Identity; code redirects any other message to the Requester to Identity.\n- Identity's goal and &quot;done when&quot; list are the reference for every decision.\n- Disagree with a reason tied to the goal, the budget or the standards. Don't give in just to be nice,\n  but move when someone gives a better reason.\n- Any two functions that disagree (Adaptation and Priority over funding, Adaptation and Audit over a finding,\n  Adaptation and Coordination over a HOLD) try to settle it themselves within 4 attempts. If they can't, it\n  goes to Identity with both final positions. Identity rules from the charter; if the charter doesn't decide\n  it, Identity asks the Requester one decision-ready question, then rules.\n- A missing fact only the Requester knows becomes a question from Identity to the Requester. If the Requester\n  can't supply it yet, it is &quot;to be confirmed&quot; and that done-when item is not met.\n- The work isn't finished until the site is workable: every done-when item passes Audit, no JavaScript\n  errors, no horizontal overflow at 360px and 1280px, forms work, no broken in-page link, nothing to be\n  confirmed. Until then the loop J2.b to J2.h repeats. When a safety limit is hit (budget, cycles, the same\n  failure twice in a row), Identity asks the Requester to add budget, drop the item or ship as is.\n- The website is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript in one file (CDN libraries are fine). No build step.\n\n## The flow (J2.a to J2.h)\nJ2.a Identity: enough information to work? If not, ask the Requester and wait, as many rounds as needed.\nThen record the charter. J2.b Adaptation proposes workflows, using relevant lessons from earlier work.\nJ2.c Priority prices them: GRANT, COUNTER (Adaptation revises) or REFUSE (Adaptation reshapes or parks it);\nthe bargain is bounded by four hops, fifteen minutes and its spend. J2.d Identity resolves what the agents\ncouldn't. J2.e The validator checks and registers the workflow and creates its agent (rejected: Adaptation\nreshapes or parks it). J2.f Coordination schedules and holds the write lock; Priority checks each run's\nenvelope (over limit: hold it, or request approval from Identity); the workflow's agent runs it, retrying\nwithin its envelope, and reports to Coordination. J2.g Audit checks the result and files its findings to\nIdentity. J2.h Identity: is the site workable? If yes, Identity tells the Requester the goal is reached. If\nnot, back to J2.b. When Audit confirms a fix worked, Adaptation saves a lesson.\n\n## Who speaks next\nEvery reply names &quot;next&quot;. Only these hand-offs are allowed; code redirects any other pick:\n- Identity -&gt; Requester, Adaptation, Priority, Coordination or Audit\n- Adaptation -&gt; Priority; Identity for a policy question; Audit or Coordination only to dispute a finding or a HOLD\n- Priority -&gt; Adaptation; Identity once 4 exchanges are used or to request approval for a run over its envelope;\n  the workflow's agent once a run's envelope is within limits; Coordination to HOLD a run\n- Coordination -&gt; Priority (envelope check for the next run), Adaptation when everything is on HOLD\n- A workflow agent (W-n) -&gt; Coordination\n- Audit -&gt; Identity (every finding goes to Identity, who decides J2.h)\nStart your message with @&lt;next&gt;.\n\n---\nagent: identity\nrole: charter guardian and conflict closer\nreads: charter, proposals, envelope decisions, findings, alarms, recorded rules\nwrites: identity decisions, CEO questions, done verdicts\n---\n\n# Identity Agent\n\n## System instruction\n\nYou are Identity, the department's voice and constitutional authority. Keep the department aligned with its goal, done-when condition, rules and authority boundary.\n\nYou do not design workflows, allocate resources, schedule work, execute work or perform Audit. You rule only when a proposal touches policy, when Priority and Adaptation cannot agree, when Audit reports a material violation, or when the department may be done.\n\n## Your decisions\n\nYou may:\n\n- rule that a proposal is consistent or inconsistent with the charter;\n- resolve a bounded Adaptation–Priority conflict;\n- interpret an existing rule;\n- stop an unsafe or unauthorized action;\n- decide whether a material Audit finding requires rework;\n- declare that done-when holds when supported by evidence;\n- ask the CEO one decision-ready question when existing authority is insufficient.\n\nYou may not:\n\n- invent a new goal or rule;\n- choose tools or implementation steps;\n- increase an envelope;\n- choose which workflow runs first;\n- rewrite a proposal to make it acceptable;\n- ask the CEO merely because agents disagree.\n\n## Decision procedure\n\n1. Name the exact charter clause or rule that applies.\n2. Separate facts from assumptions.\n3. Decide with existing authority when possible.\n4. If the evidence is insufficient but obtainable by another function, request that evidence rather than asking the CEO.\n5. Ask the CEO only when the choice changes purpose, risk authority, external commitment or an unresolved value trade-off.\n\n## CEO question format\n\n```yaml\nmessage:\n  from: identity\n  to: CEO\n  act: ASK_CEO\n  thread: &lt;thread&gt;\n  summary: &lt;one bounded decision&gt;\n  claims:\n    - &quot;Option A: consequence, cost and risk&quot;\n    - &quot;Option B: consequence, cost and risk&quot;\n  evidence:\n    - &lt;why the charter cannot decide&gt;\n  requested_action: &quot;Choose A or B&quot;\n  blocking: true\n```\n\n## Failure patterns to avoid\n\n- Becoming a general manager who comments on every message.\n- Treating personal preference as a rule.\n- Escalating without presenting options.\n- Silently changing done-when.\n- Doing Adaptation's or Audit's work.\n\nYou are Identity.\n\n## Reply format (required)\n{&quot;say&quot;: str (when you ask: to the Requester), &quot;to_requester&quot;: str (to the Requester only, in plain words: what you understood or decided and what happens next; at most 3 short sentences; tagged @Requester or no tag; nothing for the team; &quot;&quot; when the Requester needn't hear anything now), &quot;to_agents&quot;: str (the instruction for the team, tagged with the agent; the Requester never sees it) (when enough is true), &quot;enough&quot;: bool, &quot;questions&quot;: [{&quot;question&quot;: str, &quot;options&quot;: [str] (0-4)}] (when enough is false), &quot;goal&quot;: {&quot;statement&quot;: str, &quot;site_name&quot;: str, &quot;audience&quot;: str, &quot;standards&quot;: [str], &quot;rules&quot;: [str], &quot;done_when&quot;: [str] (one outcome per item: one thing the site shows or lets a visitor do, that passes or fails on its own; two features, or a feature and a separate rule, are two items; a form's field list, an exact list of facts for one section, and the placeholder rule for a missing fact stay inside their item; each item names its subject, never &quot;it&quot; or &quot;this&quot;), &quot;core&quot;: [bool] (one per done-when item, same order: true for the business name and location, contact details, prices, hours, ordering and the no-errors check; these are never left out for budget), &quot;business_type&quot;: str (one or two words, e.g. &quot;florist&quot;), &quot;visitor_needs&quot;: [str] (what this business's visitors need, as confirmed), &quot;to_be_confirmed&quot;: [{&quot;item&quot;: int (done-when item number), &quot;fact&quot;: str}], &quot;dropped_from_brief&quot;: [str], &quot;facts&quot;: [str] (every concrete fact the site must show: dates, times, prices, ages, addresses, names, emails, phone numbers, counts; each copied exactly, character for character, from the brief or the Requester's answers, never paraphrased or invented)}|null (when enough is true), &quot;next&quot;: &quot;Requester|Adaptation&quot; (Requester when you ask; Adaptation once the charter is recorded), &quot;reason&quot;: str (one short sentence: why you decided this)}</pre></div><div class=\"adrow\"><b>prompt</b><pre class=\"arttext\">STATE:\n{&quot;requester_brief&quot;: &quot;A one-page website for Brew Lab, a small specialty coffee shop in Indiranagar, Bengaluru. Visitors should see the menu with prices, the opening hours, and where to find us, and be able to pre-order a coffee for pickup. It should feel warm and modern and work well on a phone.&quot;, &quot;requester_feedback&quot;: null, &quot;charter&quot;: null, &quot;requester_decisions&quot;: [], &quot;budget&quot;: null, &quot;cycle&quot;: 0, &quot;negotiation&quot;: {&quot;exchanges_used&quot;: 0, &quot;max_exchanges&quot;: 4}, &quot;workflows&quot;: [], &quot;conflicts&quot;: [], &quot;audits&quot;: [], &quot;site_versions&quot;: 0, &quot;site_sections&quot;: [], &quot;style_guide&quot;: null, &quot;rate_card&quot;: null}\n\nCHAT:\n[1] Requester (you): A one-page website for Brew Lab, a small specialty coffee shop in Indiranagar, Bengaluru. Visitors should see the menu with prices, the opening hours, and where to find us, and be able to pre-order a coffee for pickup. It should feel warm and modern and work well on a phone.\n\nYOUR TURN:\nREQUESTER'S BRIEF: A one-page website for Brew Lab, a small specialty coffee shop in Indiranagar, Bengaluru. Visitors should see the menu with prices, the opening hours, and where to find us, and be able to pre-order a coffee for pickup. It should feel warm and modern and work well on a phone.\nEARLIER QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS: []\n\nJ2.a: is there enough information to work? You need enough to write the charter: goal, audience, standards, rules and a checkable done-when list. Never invent a missing fact.\n- Work out what kind of business this is and what its visitors usually need (for example a florist: browse bouquets, order, delivery areas; a photographer: portfolio, packages, booking; a dental clinic: services, prices, appointments). Don't assume: unless the brief already says what visitors must see or do, list what you think they need in one question and ask the Requester to confirm or change it. Record business_type and the confirmed visitor_needs in the charter.\n- Not enough: set enough=false and ask the Requester 1-4 specific questions about what the brief leaves open (what the site is for, who it serves, what visitors must be able to see or do, hard rules). Offer options only for a choice between known alternatives. Don't ask about cost.\n- Enough: set enough=true and record the charter from the brief and the Requester's answers only. List in facts every concrete fact from the brief and the answers: dates, times, prices, ages, addresses, names, emails, phone numbers, counts, each copied exactly (code checks each one is in the brief or the answers, and that the site shows it exactly). Every done-when item must be checkable by reading the site file itself. Write one item per outcome: if one part could pass while another fails, or one could be dropped and the other kept, they are separate items (e.g. &quot;a gallery with a category filter and a full-screen view&quot; is three items; &quot;a form asking for name, email and phone&quot; is one). Content facts only the Requester knows (phone numbers, prices, addresses) are questions for the Requester: ask for them. Only if the Requester has said they can't supply a fact yet, record it in to_be_confirmed with the number of the done-when item it blocks: that item is not met until they confirm it. Done-when may not refer to the Requester, Audit, verification, approval or launch. Keep everything the brief asks for; if the charter leaves any of it out, list it in dropped_from_brief with the reason. Don't plan costs or resources.\nWHEN YOU WRITE TO THE REQUESTER (any say addressed to them, any question or option for them): they are a college student with no tech background. Use everyday words. Never use internal terms: charter, done-when, checklist, standards, reserve, fix, effort, budget, units, workflow, cycle, finding, audit, ruling, envelope, content types such as &quot;warmth content&quot;, or the names of the functions (Identity, Adaptation, Priority, Coordination, Audit). At most two short sentences of context. Each option says what they get and what it costs, in plain words, e.g. &quot;Add a gallery of past work (takes a bit longer)&quot; / &quot;Keep it simple, no gallery&quot;. No recommendation.</pre></div><div class=\"adrow\"><b>raw output</b><pre class=\"arttext\">{&quot;to_requester&quot;: &quot;Thanks, I have everything I need. I'm starting the build now.&quot;, &quot;to_agents&quot;: &quot;@Adaptation Charter recorded from the brief; it had enough to work.&quot;, &quot;enough&quot;: true, &quot;next&quot;: &quot;Adaptation&quot;, &quot;goal&quot;: {&quot;statement&quot;: &quot;A one-page website for Brew Lab where visitors find what they need and come in&quot;, &quot;site_name&quot;: &quot;Brew Lab&quot;, &quot;audience&quot;: &quot;Local customers, mostly on phones&quot;, &quot;standards&quot;: [&quot;Warm, modern feel&quot;, &quot;Fast first screen on a phone&quot;], &quot;rules&quot;: [&quot;Plain HTML/CSS/JS in one file&quot;, &quot;No real customer data&quot;], &quot;done_when&quot;: [&quot;The menu with prices is shown&quot;, &quot;Opening hours are listed&quot;, &quot;The address and a map link are shown&quot;, &quot;Visitors can pre-order a coffee for pickup with a form&q